#: R/ega.b.R
msgid "The CFA could not be fitted in this sample (see the text above)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The CFA indices come from the same data that produced the EGA solution, so they favor it. Use them as a second view, and confirm with the random split in the Confirmatory Factor Analysis box."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The CFA uses the same sample as EGA, so its fit is optimistic. Tick Split the sample at random for a check in an independent subsample."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The Confirmatory factor analysis section below compares the higher-order and bifactor models with the models without hierarchy."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The EGA solution has the lowest TEFI (%s) among the structures compared, so by entropy fit it is the structure that best separates the items into dimensions."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The Expand check is EGA's original unidimensionality test: it adds simulated variables that form a separate dimension and estimates the network again. If every real item ends up in one community, the items are treated as unidimensional. Use it to reproduce results from studies that used the original EGA procedure."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The Leading eigenvalue check applies the leading eigenvalue community algorithm to the zero-order correlation matrix: a single community means one dimension (Golino et al., 2020; see references). It is an established alternative to the Louvain check."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The NCT needs complete data, so each comparison uses only the cases with every item answered."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The Network Comparison Test (NCT) compares two whole networks: whether their connections differ, and whether the total strength of their connections differs (van Borkulo et al., 2023; see references)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The agreement with the population (median ARI = %s) is lower because the population mixes the groups."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The analysis uses %d bootstrap samples with seed %d. More samples give more precise stability estimates; 500 is the usual minimum for reporting. The same seed with the same data and options always reproduces the same results; report it with your findings."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The bifactor model usually fits best because it is very flexible and also absorbs noise and response styles. Prefer it only when the general factor carries most of the common variance and the specific factors keep clear loadings; otherwise its better fit is not evidence of a general factor."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The bootstrap checks whether the dimensions found above are a stable property of the items or an accident of this particular sample (Christensen & Golino, 2021; see references)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The comparison between independent groups needs at least two groups with 10 or more cases each, so it was not run."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The confirmatory model built from this structure shows %s fit in %s: CFI = %s, RMSEA = %s (Confirmatory factor analysis)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The confirmatory model has an inadmissible solution in %s: %s (Confirmatory factor analysis)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The estimated network has no edges, so centrality is not defined."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The estimated network has no edges, so its stability cannot be assessed."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The estimated network has no edges."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The first derivative is the rate of change of each item from one time point to the next. It is dynEGA's default and the most studied choice (Golino et al., 2022; see references): the dimensions group items that rise and fall together."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The following items do not vary in the data and cannot enter the analysis: %s. Remove them."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The following items have fewer than two observed values and cannot enter a correlation matrix: %s. Remove them from the analysis."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The following items have zero variance (every respondent gave the same answer), so their correlations are undefined: %s. Remove them from the analysis; a constant item carries no information about dimensional structure."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The general factor explains %s of the common variance (explained common variance, ECV = %s). Below .70, the specific factors keep an important part of the common variance, so the scale is not essentially unidimensional."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The general factor explains %s of the common variance (explained common variance, ECV = %s). From .70 upward, the items are close to unidimensional: the specific factors add little, and a single score is often defensible."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The grouping variable has %d groups, so every pair of groups is compared separately. With many pairs, some differences will appear by chance: focus on differences that repeat across pairs or that survive the correction for multiple tests."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The groups differ from the population structure: %s (Structure of each group)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The hierarchical EGA could not be estimated, so the hierarchical models were not fitted."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The hierarchical EGA does not find a higher-order level (Hierarchical EGA)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The hierarchical EGA finds %d higher-order dimensions above %d lower-order dimensions (Hierarchical EGA)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The hierarchical EGA finds a general higher-order dimension above %d lower-order dimensions (Hierarchical EGA)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The hierarchical EGA found no higher-order level, so the higher-order model is not fitted."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The higher-order model fits exactly as well as the correlated lower-order factors: with two or three lower-order factors under one higher-order factor, the higher level only re-expresses their correlations. The data cannot tell the two apart; the choice rests on theory and on how the scores will be used."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The higher-order model reaches CFI %s against %s for the correlated lower-order factors. The loss is larger than .01: under this descriptive criterion the higher-order factors do not reproduce the correlations among the lower-order factors well, so the model without hierarchy is preferable."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The higher-order model reaches CFI %s against %s for the correlated lower-order factors. The loss of fit is limited (.01 or less) under this descriptive criterion, and the model is more restrictive; interpreting the structure as hierarchical requires further evaluation (theory, the loadings of the higher-order factors and replication in another sample)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The highest CFI is that of %s (%s)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The items form %d dimensions: %s (Exploratory Graph Analysis)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The items form a single dimension (Exploratory Graph Analysis)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The least central items: %s. Weakly connected items add little to the network; check their network loadings and their stability."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The level (derivative 0) is the smoothed value of each item within the window. Its structure is closest to that of the raw scores and keeps stable differences between occasions; choose it to describe states rather than change."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The lower-order dimensions are the same as in the EGA solution above."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The lower-order dimensions group into %s higher-order dimensions, which suggests broader domains above the specific dimensions."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The lower-order level has %s dimensions against %s in the EGA solution above, and places %d of %d items as EGA does. Items in a different dimension: %s. The lower level of Louvain tends to split broad dimensions, so this is expected."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The lowest BIC, which balances fit and the number of parameters, is that of %s (%s)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The mean width of the edge intervals is %s. Two edges whose intervals overlap widely cannot be said to differ in strength."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The median agreement with the population membership is high (ARI = %s) and most persons show the population's number of dimensions, so the population structure is a reasonable summary of individuals."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The median agreement with the population membership is low (ARI = %s): individual structures differ markedly from the population structure, so it should not be read as the structure of each person. This pattern is consistent with a non-ergodic process, although individual networks with few time points are also noisy."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The median agreement with the population membership is moderate (ARI = %s): the population structure describes individuals only partly. Report the individual heterogeneity together with the population result."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The most central items by strength are %s (Centrality)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The most frequent number of dimensions appears in only %s of the bootstrap samples, below the %s cutoff (Stability)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The most frequent number of dimensions in the bootstrap is %d, in %s of the samples, and differs from the %d estimated in the data (Stability)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The most frequent solution appears in only %s of samples, below the %s cutoff, so the number of dimensions itself is not firmly established: neighbouring solutions are also common. Report the full frequency distribution, not only the most frequent value."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The network above was estimated with the %s model, but the NCT always uses the graphical lasso. The networks compared here may therefore differ slightly from the one above."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The network comparison test finds no global difference between %s (Group comparison)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The network structure differs: the largest difference in a single connection (M = %s) is larger than expected by chance (%s). At least one relation between items is not the same in the two networks. The edge test (Test every edge) looks for which ones; after its correction for many tests, a single edge may not reach significance even when M does, so read M as evidence that the networks differ somewhere."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The networks of %s differ in %s (Group comparison)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The number of dimensions could not be computed (Exploratory Graph Analysis)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The order of the items by %s is moderately stable (CS = %s): interpret only large differences between items, and with care."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The order of the items by %s is not stable (CS = %s): it changes when only a few cases are dropped, so differences between items should not be interpreted."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The order of the items by %s is stable (CS = %s): it holds when up to %s of the cases are dropped, so differences between items can be interpreted."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The random-intercept EGA did not converge and cannot be interpreted (Random-intercept EGA)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The random-intercept EGA ran without items marked as negatively worded, so it does not show a wording effect; read it only as a sensitivity check (Random-intercept EGA)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The random-intercept model did not converge, so this result cannot be interpreted: non-convergence is not evidence that there is no wording effect, nor that there is one. riEGA then runs EGA on the ordinary correlations, so the riEGA column repeats an ordinary EGA without any wording control. Check the direction of the items and the sample size before drawing conclusions about wording."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The riEGA TEFI is computed on the residual correlations, so it is not comparable with the TEFI of the EGA solution above."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The same number of dimensions appeared in %s of the bootstrap samples (Stability)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The second derivative is the acceleration of each item. It captures how changes speed up or slow down together, but it amplifies measurement noise and needs many time points per person; use it only with dense series."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The stability of the structure was not checked. Without the bootstrap, it is unknown whether the same number of dimensions would appear in another sample (Stability)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The structure can be estimated for the whole sample (population), for each group and for each person. Comparing them shows whether one structure describes everyone or whether people differ."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The theoretical structure and the EGA solution group the items in the same way, which supports the intended structure."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The theoretical structure has no item of the analysis in any factor."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The theoretical structure includes variables that are not among the Items of the analysis: %s. Add them to Items or remove them from the factors."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The theoretical structure places %d of %d items as EGA. The items EGA places elsewhere (%s) are the ones to examine: read their content and check whether they are also unstable in the bootstrap or redundant in UVA."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "Theoretical"
msgstr ""

#: ega/options/theoryFactors.title
#: ega/ui[0][2].label
msgid "Theoretical structure"
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "Theoretical structure: build it as in jamovi's Confirmatory Factor Analysis. Drag the items of each dimension into a factor, use Add New Factor for the next one, and click a factor's name to rename it. Items left out of every factor are not part of the theoretical structure."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "There are no negative connections, so expected influence orders the items exactly as strength does."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "There is no established cutoff for a meaningful TEFI difference. Read the differences together with the bootstrap stability, the CFA fit and the content of the items that move, and report the TEFI of every structure compared."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "There is no evidence that the network structure differs: the largest difference in a single connection (M = %s) is within what is expected by chance (%s). This does not prove the networks are equal; with small groups the test has little power."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "These factor names are used more than once: %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "These items are in more than one factor of the theoretical structure: %s. Each item can belong to one factor only."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "These models are fitted on the same data that produced the EGA and hierEGA solutions, so their fit is optimistic. Report them as a comparison, and confirm the chosen model in a new sample."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "These variables look like measurement schedule variables rather than responses, because nearly every person has the same value at the same measurement number: %s. Examples are the day or the beep number. Time variables change together by design, so they form a dimension of their own. Remove them from Items, and use one of them as Time if it orders the measurements."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "This result is valid only if the negatively worded items are in their original, unrecoded direction and the scale mixes both directions. If all items are worded in the same direction, do not read it as a wording effect."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "This section checks how accurate the estimated network is, with the bootnet procedures (Epskamp et al., 2018; see references). It re-estimates the same network as EGA (same correlation, model and missing-value handling) in many bootstrap samples."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "This section compares the network of the items between groups of people. With a grouping variable it compares independent groups (for example, women and men, or two countries). With a second measurement of the same items it compares the same people at two moments (for example, before and after an intervention)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "This section compares the structure found by EGA with alternative structures for the same items, using the Total Entropy Fit Index (TEFI; Golino et al., 2021; see references). Every structure is evaluated on the same correlation matrix as the EGA above, so their TEFI values can be compared directly."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "This summary gathers the main results of the sections below, what remains uncertain and what needs attention. Each line points to the section that explains it; read those sections before drawing conclusions."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "This table opens up the bifactor model of the comparison above: for each item, its loading on the general factor (every item) and on its specific factor (the hierEGA lower-order dimension), and its residual variance. It shows whether the specific factors keep their own variance or collapse into the general factor, which is the usual source of inadmissible bifactor solutions (Rodriguez, Reise & Haviland, 2016; see references)."
msgstr ""

